   "transcript": "Presenter: Mary Wallace ‘Wally’ Funk [is] an American aviator who passed rigorous tests to become one of the Mercury 13, as part of the Women in Space programme. Nearly 60 years later, Wally is still chasing her dream to go into space.\nGuest: Wally is amazing. She’s still alive, she’s 79, she’s 80 next year. She is a pilot, still flies most weekends and she was one of 13 women, also female pilots, who a very forward-thinking medical doctor called Dr Randolph Lovelace II, who’s one of the – it’s almost as good a name as Wally Funk quite frankly –\nPresenter: Almost.\nGuest: Almost, yes, had decided that he wanted to see whether the tests that he’d given the men, to the test pilots that eventually became the first American pilots, the Mercury 7, he wanted to see if women could pass those same tests, and not only could they pass them, in many cases they passed those tests better than the men and as the staff at the clinic that administered the tests said, ‘they complained less than the men’.\nAnd Wally was the youngest of these 13 women so that’s why they’re called the Mercury 13 now, because they took the same tests as the Mercury 7. It wasn’t funded by NASA but they were – the tests were administered by Randolph Lovelace who did work for NASA – so exactly the same tests. The women’s tests were privately funded, partly by Jackie Cochran, who was the first woman to break the sound barrier.\nPresenter: Things changed and Jackie Cochran changed her tune a bit, didn’t she?\nGuest: Yes, it’s quite hard to get your head around really because here was a woman who was a trailblazer in her own right. She helped fund the programme that got these women to, to be in a position where they could have been astronauts, but NASA wouldn’t admit women at the time. And then there was a committee hearing when the programme was very abruptly cancelled and it lost its funding, and these women had taken the test between 1960 and 61. That’s how far ahead they were. They were ready to fly and go into space and be part of the moon programme.\nBut Jackie Cochran, at a committee hearing, actually, she was supposed to be on their side but actually sort of spoke in a way that, that furthered the defence because she said ‘well yes, maybe women are, you know, should go into space but maybe not now’ and John Glenn, the astronaut, he said that putting women into space was against the social order, and they lost their case and so women weren’t admitted into NASA until the late 70s and the first woman didn’t fly until 1983, Sally Ride.\nPresenter: That, of course, goes without saying. We need to mention Valentina Tereshkova −\nGuest: Absolutely!\nPresenter: Who did – the Russian cosmonaut who did indeed go into space.\nGuest: And beat the women. They were there and ready. I mean, they were delighted for her obviously, it was a woman, but they were a bit upset because she wasn’t a pilot.\nPresenter: No, she wasn’t a pilot.\nGuest: They wanted a pilot.\nPresenter: Look, I want to get a flavour, more of a flavour of Wally Funk. Here she is, here’s a clip of Wally. She went to a school called St Stephens College, Stephens College, and her mother got a phone call when she was 15.\nWally Funk: ‘My adviser, Dr Bates, called Mother up and said, “Mrs Funk, we need to chat. Your daughter is not doing as well as she could in her classwork.” And Mother said, “Dr Bates, do you have an airport?” He said, “Yes.” “Then you get her out there tomorrow.” And he did. And that’s when I started flying.’\nInterviewer: ‘Did your grades improve?’\nWally Funk: ‘I don’t remember. Because I was either at the gym or the airport. And then I could wear pants because every night, you had to have heels, hose and dress, frou-frou stuff and a skirt. If I was going flying, I could wear pants, not Levi’s but pants.’\nGuest: I love hearing her voice. It really – ah, it’s wonderful to hear her."
